WEBVTT

00:00:00.450 --> 00:00:04.879
GEM four D. Geometry enhanced video world
models for robot manipulation.

00:00:06.450 --> 00:00:10.941
During training, a frozen geometry teacher
provides features. An auxiliary branch

00:00:10.941 --> 00:00:15.840
learns to predict them from video features,
sending geometric supervision back into

00:00:15.840 --> 00:00:17.065
the video model.

00:00:18.450 --> 00:00:23.488
At inference, remove the teacher and
geometry branch. Their training signal
improves

00:00:23.488 --> 00:00:28.106
the video backbone without adding geometry
branch computation to video generation.

00:00:29.450 --> 00:00:34.709
Our latest cloth rollout shows predicted
RGB, estimated depth, and scene flow side

00:00:34.709 --> 00:00:35.518
by side.

00:00:37.450 --> 00:00:42.509
The can task tests a different interaction:
contact followed by a change in the

00:00:42.509 --> 00:00:43.232
object’s orientation.

00:00:44.450 --> 00:00:48.829
The toy task follows a longer sequence, from
grasping and lifting to placement in

00:00:48.829 --> 00:00:49.454
the bowl.

00:00:50.950 --> 00:00:56.255
An adaptive inverse dynamics system grounds
the scene in three dimensions, tracks

00:00:56.255 --> 00:01:00.675
the end effector, repairs unreliable poses,
and synthesizes an executable

00:01:00.675 --> 00:01:01.117
trajectory.

00:01:02.450 --> 00:01:07.264
Here is the predicted cup pickup, alongside
the recorded execution on a real robot.

00:01:08.450 --> 00:01:13.212
For the red pepper, compare the generated
pickup with the recorded robot execution.

00:01:14.450 --> 00:01:19.388
Across seven RL Bench tasks, mean execution
success rises from twenty two point

00:01:19.388 --> 00:01:24.705
seven to seventy five point one percent.
Both video models use the same action

00:01:24.705 --> 00:01:25.465
extraction system.

00:01:26.950 --> 00:01:31.927
Explore the paper, code, and demonstrations
on the GEM four D project page.

